Date: Thu, 27 May 93 23:56:25 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PRESS: Space Colonies etc unlikely according to AP

Phil Fraering takes great exception to an article about the expansion of
life throughout the galaxy. The article appears to be mostly a restatement
of the Fermi Paradox ("Why aren't they here?," as regard the aliens who
should've expanded across the galaxy by now) and doesn't seem to me to be
"propaganda" at all.

>The following is reprinted without permission to for the benefit
>of the private e-mail list "extropians" so that they may know
>that they live in an age of abominable propaganda that would put
>Pravda to shame.
>
>Anyone want to "list the fallacies"?


>AP Science editor.
>
>{Phil here: I'm putting my comments in curlicues. That we have
>a "science editor" of the AP that believes this says a lot about
>the "freedom of the press" in this country: it may be free, but
>it'll still put Pravda to shame}

What does having a "Science editor" for a private news agency (AP is a
competitor of Reuters, etc.) have to do with freedom of the press? I note
with interest that "Extropy" also has a Science Editor, Dave Krieger. 
....
>According to the calculations of a Princeton University
>astrophysicist, if we were ever going to roam the galaxy, the odds are
>we'd be doing it already.

I presume the author is not implying "we," as in the current inhabitants of
Earth, as that is clearly counterfactual. I presume the meaning is
"intelligent beings." Taken this way, this is the standard Fermi Paradox,
as noted above. Cf. Barrow and Tipler's "The Anthopic Cosmological
Principle" for a full explication.

...Drake-equation-like stuff elided....

>That's where the conclusion about colonization comes from. If we were
>to colonize the galaxy's estimated 1 billion hospitable planets {Phil
>here; where did they get that figure?}, the human population would
>expand enormously. It could be a billion times what it is now.
>
>Most human beings who ever lived or ever would live would be among
>them. The odds are overwhelming that we would be, too.

Note that this is precisely the scenario we ourselves (the Extropians) are
predicting: that we will expand into the galaxy roughly as stated in this
article. That we haven't found _others_ filling the galaxy (and perhaps
building Dyson spheres or other detectable artifacts, such as von Neumann
machines replicating themselves across the galaxy in a few million years)
is fairly persuasive evidence (to me at least) that human-like (or any
sapiens-like) civilizations are relatively rare (as in only a handful or
less per galaxy in the last few billion years).

.........Drake-equation-like stuff elided again.... (what Gott appears to
be doing in his work is updating part of Drake's equations and making some
very rough estimates of how long typical civilizations live. We can all
point to possible errors, but the exercise is not necessarily a bad thing
to pursue--in time, the estimates may become more precise....we debated
this business a couple of weeks ago. Ironically, several of us went to see
Drake speak a few days later.)...........

>One implication of the findings is that we might want to encourage
>colonization of space, Gott said, "not just explore and come back."

Hear hear! This is exactly right! Given the chance of "xyz" annihilation on
Earth, which even Extropians will surely agree is a possibility, it
behooves us to get off the planet as soon as we can, at least to create
colonies. Gott is clearly supporting this highly Extropian goal.

....
>Gott's findings are based on the assumption that most human beings
>live somewhere in the middle of the period of human existance.
>
>Only a very small minority of people live near the beginning of human
>existance. So the odds are we aren't among them.

The editor/writer is unfortunately using the word "people," which may be
what is confusing/angering Phil. If he had said "other civilizations," or
even "aliens," the meaning would be clearer. But Gott's basic point is
correct: the law of averages (law of large numbers, actually) makes it
likely that if there are N civilizations developed in a galaxy in the
first, say, 10-20 billion years, then we humans can expect *not* to have
been the first, nor the last, nor any other special place. (It's certainly
possible we're the first...someone has to be...but it's ipso facto
unlikely). Roughly N/2 of these civilizations would've come before us, and
roughly N/2 after us...this is undoubtedly what was meant by the comments
above. (I won't really blast the editor either, as it takes more space to
explain these points than perhaps the story was given. Unfortunate, but
that's science writing for you.)

.....some of Phil's comments elided for brevity...
Phil continues:
>But the truth is, Gott's premise is a complete perversion of the idea
>of symmetry: he's saying there _is_ something special about this
>moment in time, because it _must_ be the midpoint in human history.
>Back to the regularly scheduled agitprop.}

Gott is apparently just making a variant of the point I just made, that we
should be somewhere "in the pack," not necessarily at the exact midpoint
(I'd be stunned if Gott said anywhere we are at the exact N/2 point, even
if we could ever define it!). If N is about a million, then we would expect
many thousands of civilizations to have reached space-faring levels before
us, on average. And they would have reached this level hundreds of millions
of years ago, on average. Plenty of time to colonize, launch von Neumann
probes, build artifacts, etc. (These are very general arguments, as we
cannot estimate N, or the lifetime of civilizations, etc. It sounds to me
that Gott is, like Drake, attempting to estimate the lifetime of
civilizations given some assumptions about N and given that we see no signs
of alien civilizations around us. This is a large topic, which I'll leave
for now.)

.....more stuff elided....
>Notice how the last two paragraphs, where "Gott has the numbers" and
>the random factoid are presented, supposedly are supposed to make
>everyone accept this, roll over, and die.
>
>As Homey the Clown would say: "I don't think so."
>
>In the meantime, I'm not sure I can trust anything AP or "Nature"
>says...

I still cherish "Nature." I think the article, if this was all of it (in
Phil's post, not this edited-down one), was a bit too short to convey the
essence of the Fermi Paradox and the Anthropic Cosmological Principle to
newspaper readers (what the AP is digestifying for), but the article is not
nearly as propagandistic as Phil is claiming, in my opinion. In fact, some
Extropians (me, for example) have argued these same sorts of points before.

Gott didn't seem to be grinding an axe for the Greens, or the anti-nukes,
or One Worlders, so I don't see the reason for calling it agitprop. As for
the editor's seeming slap against Trekkies, that's just the "hook" used by
modern journalists. And the point about Trekkies is even on-target, even if
not intentionally--the "Star Trek" vision of ubiquitious civilizations,
with nearly every star system having a civilization that is within a few
thousand years of the current level of Earth, is the most misleading
scenario ever presented. I sometimes enjoy "Star Trek," but its vision of a
universe filled with civilizations (which we've so far been unvisited by)
is wildly implausible.

My personal estimate is that homo sapiens is the ONLY bunch of
intelligences (above reptiles, for example) anywhere in the entire galaxy!
Not necessarily because other civilizations evolved and then destroyed
themselves, but becaue no others have even evolved yet.

Gott's point of view, from what I can tell, seems fairly close to mine.

Phil may disagree, but I hope we can debate this very interesting issue
without getting too emotionally tied to specific positions. For some
reason, Phil got extremely annoyed at this article, which detracted from
whatever real critique he may have had in mind.

Onward and outward, as they say!

-Tim May




