Date:     Fri, 28 May 93 7:29:02 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON/SPACE: Fiat Homesteading

There's still no alternative to homesteading the Universe, regardless of
how much difficulty anyone may have understanding this.  The only
alternatives are in the way of who takes first possession.

Robin's argument seems to go as follows: have the world-state (United
Nations) take first possession of the entire universe by fiat, then
proceed to divide it up amongst people.  There is some optimal way that
this can be done by the world-state, some reason to believe that the
world-state can be trusted to do so...

Then he proposes some confusing method by which the world-state is supposed
to divvy up the universe.

This is still homesteading, just by the world-state, rather than private
parties.  (Private insofar as they aren't coercive monopolies, like the
world-state.)  As such, it still begs the question.  If the problem is
how to establish ownership of the universe, then this solution requires
merely collectivizing the universe first by fiat, then somehow privatizing
it.  Why collectivize it?  Why not let pioneers take first posession
for themselves?  Are we so lacking in interventionist public policy that
we've got to go around making up more things for the statists to seize?!?

As for the difficulty of determining first use of a property, I agree with
Dan Davis that this would be imperfect but it would be less so than the
"reasonable use" standard.  Rather than only having to determine whether
A used X, it would also have to be determined whether A's use of X was
"reasonable."  Occam's razor would seem to support keeping it simply to
the factual question of use, excluding the evaluative question of
reasonableness.

