>I propose that the United Nations, or most nations that signed
>previous space treaties, sign a new treaty agreeing to auction the
>universe from the moon on out as a single unit (excepting rights to
>near earth sunlight).

In other words, have those organized criminals comprising the UN declare by
arbitrary fiat that they own the whole goddamn universe and threaten to back
this claim up with force if any honest person claims any of it for themselves.

This is outrageous.  We've already got these protection rackets claiming
the oceans for themselves, and, as if that weren't enough, Robin wants them
to seize everything else as well!

>The universe owner would eventually break up the universe into smaller
>chunks and auction off these chunks, and these new universe-part
>owners could similarly auction off smaller chunks.  To discourage
>monopolies, a rule might be added that no commercial use could be made
>of any chunk until it had been sold at an auction where no owner held
>more than 10% (or X%) of the market value of the chunks sold at that
>auction open to all comers.

Why would the owner sell anything?  Why not simply collect monopoly rents
from the rest of us?  The bit about discouraging monopolies is the height
of absurdity!  Robin, you propose to BEGIN with a coercive monopoly, for
goodness sakes!  Not even a natural one, but a statist, collectivist,
coercive one.

>But in better cases, the value to us of the universe existing out
>there is not entirely spent in a race to get there first, and we can
>all be better off.

Right.  Quite clearly we'd all be better off if the United Statists of Earth
threatened to nuke anyone that tried to claim any of their universe without
their permission, then did what they damn well pleased - they'd have no
reason whatsoever to implement any elegant academic privatization scheme.

Gee, I'm learning more from Jeff Hummell than I thought: see whether there
actually is a market failure rather than a government failure; see whether
it's possible to know how to "solve" the failure; see whether it's possible
to set up institutions so that they will have the incentives to solve it;
and then see whether the establishment of such institutions will be stable,
and not threaten to impose more costs upon anyone than they are benefited.

Robin's merely assumed, not argued, that homesteading is a market failure
and that it's possible for government to solve it, and hasn't even taken
into consideration at all the knowledge, incentive, or institutional
stability problems.

How can we know that homesteading is a market failure?  How can we know that
its costs will be greater than those of an organized crime racket that won't
have the correct incentives or institutional stability even if it could
possibly solve the knowledge problem?

