Robin may have started a good thing here! Shamelessly ripping
off even his wording for my own evil purposes:

PROBLEM:  All those wonderful undiscovered ideas, methods, products,
physical laws, theorems, and so on, out there -- without an accepted
route to ownership, people won't invest in getting there.

SOLUTION:  Homestead stuff.  Whoever gets to the ideas and discoveries
first and "claims" them becomes accepted owner.

PROBLEM:  This creates a commons in getting there first.  So too much
is wasted getting there too fast.  We'd all be better off waiting.
SSC indeed!  University research labs!  Lives ruined by fevered
jealousy toward intermittent windshield wipers!  Peh!

SOLUTION:  Auction off stuff.  Give the proceeds out on a per-IQ
basis, or whatever is politically feasible (i.e. give it all to the
large corporations that can afford the lobbyists and lawyers or into
government trust [sic] funds).  The important thing is that ideas
get *owned*.

PROBLEM:  How to define all ideas so that everything new will fall
into some "location" in the divided-up thought landscape?

SOLUTION:  Well, we're good at building taxonomic systems... one
could imagine dividing technology up by the physical effects required
to produce it and prerequisite technologies... physical law could
be similarly divided and certainly mathematical law could be
done so as well.  Physics and math have the "new axiom" problem to
contend with; math can come up with classification schemes for
new axioms... physics can just plan for more of them becoming
available later... say, eight new fundamental physical laws, by
order of discovery.  Speculative territory, but the market knows
how to deal with that!  The ninth and later new fundamental
physical laws can be left as a "frontier" to placate our whining
descendents who unreasonably complain that we grabbed everything
before they were born...

This needs work, but so does anything worthwhile.

