 pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu ("Phil G. Fraering") writes:
>But the truth is, Gott's premise is a complete perversion of the idea
>of symmetry: he's saying there _is_ something special about this
>moment in time, because it _must_ be the midpoint in human history.
>Back to the regularly scheduled agitprop.}

 He is saying we are probably within a few standard deviations of the midpoint.
This is a reasonable conclusion given the symmetry premise. That premise is
arbitrary at best. Barrow and Tipler have made a pretty compelling argument
(in The Anthropic Cosmological Principle) that the symmetry principle is
incompatible with the theory that the universe started with the big bang
or any other unique starting point. They also give a fairly strong argument
that the absence of other life in our galaxy is incompatible with any theory
that implies frequent opportunities for life to have evolved in our causal
past. I am aware of only one cosmological theory that appears to be consistent
with both the symmetry principle and our observations (the "eternal inflationary
universe" mentioned in Barrow's Theories of Everything, a book of which I have
submitted a review to Extropy).

