From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: PRESS: Space Colonies etc unlikely according to AP

I think what Gott is saying is that _humans_ will never colonize the
universe.  (Note - I am basing this just on Phil's post.)

When I was a boy, I would often wonder, "why was I born here and now,
instead of at some other time throughout the whole sweep of history?"

Gott is arguing that it is because back 1000 years ago, the population of
earth was a tiny fraction of what it is today.  Therefore the odds are
overwhelming that I will find myself alive today rather than 1000 years
ago.

He extends this argument to say that _if_ human population eventually grew
to a billion times its present size, then the chances that I would find
myself alive today are only one in a billion.  The odds are overwhelming
that I would have been born during that era when the population was largest.
Since I was born today and not 1000 years in the future, it "follows" that
the population will never be much larger than it is today (and perhaps that
the population will soon be much smaller than it currently is).

It's an "anthropic" style argument, but directed towards our personal
probabilities of being alive at some time.  Note that the argument would
be valid for most people who exist throughout all of human history.

Like all anthropic arguments, it requires a certain sense of counterfactuals
(the universe "could" have had no electrons, why does it have them?)
(I "could" have been born any time, why today?) that people may or may not
be comfortable with.

Hal

