Date: Fri, 28 May 93 08:50:24 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON/IDEAS: Auction All Intellectual Property!

I think Derek has a good point that the aggressive exploration and colonization
that develops in a homesteading system, actions which the economists tell
us are "dissipating" some of the potential economic value of the property,
do seem analogous to the aggressive competition to get ahead in a capitalist
economy.

We celebrate this competition and we can see many values from having businesses
have to work against each other to acquire customers and develop a successful
product.  But it does lead to a lot of personal hardship as people are driven
to take chances and behave entrepreneurially in the hopes of becoming the
wealthy owner of a successful business.  Most businesses fail, often taking
with them the life savings of their hopeful founders.

This kind of aggressive desperation with attendant suffering and disappointment
is considered to be a bad thing in the case of homesteading.  It causes
people to develop properties "too soon", and most of them never become rich
as the hoped-for increased value happens only after they have spent enough
years of suffering that they could have earned that value anyway.

I wonder if there is an inconsistency between support for entrepreneurial
capitalism and opposition to unrestricted homesteading.

Hal

